    "seo": {
      "title": "Food Travel in Iran: Regional Kitchens and Living Heritage",
      "description": "Discover Iranian food through regional ingredients, rice and bread traditions, local cooking knowledge, and the people behind the meal."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "reading-the-country-through-food",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A cuisine with many local expressions",
        "content": "Iranian food is best approached through both shared culinary habits and differences between places. Mohammad Reza Ghanoonparvar's account in Encyclopaedia Iranica links this diversity to climate, agricultural resources, and ethnic traditions, while noting that many dishes recur across the country with regional and individual variations. A familiar name therefore need not imply an identical preparation. [s1]\n\nFor a visitor, this suggests a useful starting question: what changes when a dish moves between kitchens? Its grain, souring ingredient, accompanying herbs, or preparation method may reveal more than a list of famous dishes. Regional food travel becomes an exercise in comparison, with the cook's explanation helping to connect a meal to its setting. This is an editorial approach to the variation documented in the culinary literature, rather than a claim that every locality has a fixed repertoire. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "understanding-the-menu",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Rice, bread, and the structure of a meal",
        "content": "Several terms make menus easier to interpret. **Chelow** is plain steamed rice; **polow** commonly combines rice with other ingredients; **kateh** is cooked by absorbing the water. **Khoresh** denotes a stew or sauce, while **ash** refers to a family of substantial soups. These distinctions direct attention to preparation and how dishes are served together. [s1]\n\nFruit, herbs, and nuts are important beyond dessert or garnish. Culinary accounts describe fruit in savory dishes and nuts used whole, chopped, or ground. Looking for these combinations opens a broader view of Iranian cooking than a meal organized entirely around grilled meat. It also makes comparisons productive: a sour component or a ground-nut sauce can become the thread connecting otherwise different dishes. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "regional-ingredients",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Follow an ingredient to its landscape",
        "content": "The inspected publisher's account of Najmieh Batmanglij's regional research offers several concrete geographical connections: saffron in Khorasan, pistachios in Kerman, dates around the Persian Gulf, and tea and rice beside the Caspian. These examples are useful orientation points, not exclusive boundaries around where an ingredient can grow or be eaten. [s2]\n\nThe same account describes fish purchased in Bushehr and prepared with tamarind, cilantro, and garlic, alongside a very different Isfahan preparation combining lamb, yogurt, saffron, and citrus peel. The contrast illustrates the value of asking how ingredients are assembled, rather than assuming that one national seasoning profile explains every meal. The publisher's description supports these examples; the full cookbook's recipes have not been inspected for this page. [s2]\n\nThe branches below allow closer exploration of Gilan, Azerbaijani, southern, and Khorasan cuisines, alongside sweets and food routes. These are navigation categories, not a complete classification of Iran's overlapping culinary traditions.",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "food-as-shared-practice",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Look beyond the finished dish",
        "content": "UNESCO's account of the multinational flatbread-making and sharing tradition includes communities in Iran alongside neighboring countries. It describes cooperation in preparation, several kinds of baking equipment, and bread shared during ordinary meals and life events. Its heritage significance lies in participation, transmission, and hospitality, rather than a claim that one nation exclusively owns the tradition. [s3]\n\nRasht provides an urban example of food heritage recognition: UNESCO lists it as a Creative City of Gastronomy, with membership beginning in 2015. That designation offers a starting point for exploring a city's relationship with food; it should not be treated as a rating of every restaurant. [s4]\n\nA rewarding food journey can therefore move between ingredients, techniques, and social settings. Ask what a preparation is called locally, how it differs from another version, and who taught the cook to make it. These questions leave room for variation and change while keeping the people who sustain culinary knowledge at the center of the experience. [s1][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
