    "content": [
      {
        "id": "a-regional-pattern",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A recognizable culinary pattern",
        "content": "Gilan's cooking offers a distinctive way into the food culture of Iran's Caspian region. Anthropologist Christian Bromberger describes a cuisine in which rice, garden produce, herbs, eggs, poultry, and fish recur, with sour flavors providing a characteristic accent. Vinegar and juices from unripe fruit help create that acidity. These are recurring tendencies in a diverse regional kitchen, rather than compulsory ingredients in every meal. [s1]\n\nA useful introduction is **kateh**, rice cooked by absorbing its water. It places the staple at the center of the meal and leaves accompanying dishes to supply contrasting flavors and textures. Bromberger's account also records differences between the rice-growing lowlands and drier upland areas, where bread and dairy products have had greater importance. Gilan should therefore not be reduced to the cooking of a single city or landscape. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "dishes-and-condiments",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Recognizing dishes without fixing one recipe",
        "content": "**Mirza ghasemi** combines eggplant, garlic, tomato, and egg. **Baghala ghatogh** is a bean stew associated with eggs, while **sir torshi** is garlic preserved in vinegar. These examples reveal how vegetables, eggs, and sour accompaniments can organize a meal without making meat its main point of reference. [s1]\n\nNames provide orientation rather than a complete ingredient declaration. The presence of vegetables alone does not establish that a dish contains no egg or other animal ingredients. Reading the preparations together is more informative than treating each as an isolated specialty: rice supplies the foundation, while cooked dishes and preserved accompaniments create variety. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "rasht-and-living-heritage",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Rasht, resources, and cooking knowledge",
        "content": "UNESCO's profile of Rasht links its gastronomy to the fertile Sefid-Rud delta, fish, and seasonal products. It also emphasizes inherited utensils and methods, including cooking in **gamaj** clay pots. This shifts attention from a menu of specialties to the knowledge and material equipment through which food is prepared. [s2]\n\nRasht joined UNESCO's Creative Cities Network in the gastronomy category in 2015. The profile describes training activities and presents ambitions for research, cultural exchange, and a local food museum. Those ambitions should be read as stated objectives: the profile does not, by itself, demonstrate that every proposed project was completed or measure its effects. [s2]\n\nFor a reader exploring Gilan, the strongest connection is between a dish and the resources, techniques, and people behind it. A clay cooking pot, seasonal ingredient, or sour preserve can open a conversation about how culinary knowledge survives and changes. This approach gives Rasht's recognition a practical context while leaving room for traditions elsewhere in the province. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      }
